export const reviewerRubric = `## The rubric

Check the PRD against these eight dimensions. Not every PRD needs every
dimension in full — judge by what the PRD is actually proposing — but note
explicitly when a dimension is skipped and whether that's reasonable (e.g. a
small internal tooling PRD may not need a rollout/rollback plan; say so
rather than silently ignoring the dimension).

1. **Problem statement / context** — Is it clear *why* this is being built,
   who it's for, and what happens if it isn't? A PRD that opens straight
   into a solution without stating the problem is a gap here.
2. **Success metrics** — Are there specific, measurable success criteria
   (not just "improve engagement")? Is it stated *how* and *when* they'll be
   measured?
3. **Scope** — Is what's explicitly *out* of scope stated, not just what's
   in? Ambiguous scope boundaries are one of the most common sources of
   downstream Jira ticket sprawl.
4. **Requirements with acceptance criteria** — Does each feature or user
   story have concrete, testable acceptance criteria, or just a paragraph of
   prose a reader has to interpret?
5. **Edge cases and error states** — Are the non-happy-paths addressed
   (empty states, failure modes, concurrent use, permission boundaries)?
6. **Non-functional requirements** — Performance, security, privacy, data
   retention, accessibility, compliance — anything with a real constraint
   that isn't a "feature" but will still show up as engineering work.
7. **Dependencies, risks, open questions** — Are they named, or discovered
   later? An open question is not a gap by itself — an *unacknowledged* one
   is.
8. **Rollout / rollback** — Is there a plan for how this ships (phased,
   flagged, all-at-once) and what happens if it needs to be pulled back?`;
export const reviewerOutputFormat = `## Output format

Structure the report as:

1. **One-line verdict** — ready to build from / needs targeted fixes /
   needs significant rework — stated plainly up front, not buried at the
   end.
2. **Findings grouped by severity**, each citing the specific PRD section or
   a short quote so the user can find it immediately:
   - **Blocker** — this PRD cannot responsibly be turned into engineering
     work as-is (e.g. no success metric at all, no acceptance criteria
     anywhere).
   - **Gap** — present but weak or incomplete (e.g. scope stated but one
     obvious boundary case left ambiguous).
   - **Nice-to-have** — would strengthen the PRD but isn't blocking.
3. **Closing pointer** — if there are any Blockers or Gaps, end with: "Use
   the \`improve-prd\` skill to work through these" rather than suggesting
   fixes inline here.

Do not pad the report with praise for what's already good — a short
"strengths" note is fine if genuinely notable, but the report's job is to
surface what's missing, not to reassure.`;
export const improverLoop = `## How to work through it

Don't rewrite the whole PRD at once. For each dimension the user wants to
address (typically the Blockers and Gaps \`prd-quality-reviewer\` flagged,
taken one at a time):

1. **Ask a targeted clarifying question** specific to that gap — not "tell
   me about your success metrics" but something answerable in one or two
   sentences, e.g. "What number would tell you this feature worked — and
   over what time window?"
2. **Propose concrete replacement or added text** based on the answer,
   written the way the rest of the PRD is written (match its tone and
   section structure — don't impose a different template).
3. **Show it as a before/after** — the current text (or "currently absent")
   next to the proposed text — so the user is approving a specific change,
   not a vague direction.
4. **Get explicit sign-off before writing it into the PRD file.** Move to
   the next gap only after this one is either accepted, edited, or
   explicitly deferred by the user.

If the user wants to defer a gap (e.g. "we'll figure out rollback later"),
that's a valid outcome — note it as an explicitly acknowledged open item
rather than pushing to resolve everything.`;
export const improverExample = `## Worked example

> Reviewer flagged: **Gap — Success metrics** — "the PRD says this feature
> will 'improve user engagement' with no target or measurement window."
>
> **Clarifying question:** "What number would tell you this worked, and
> over what time window — e.g. weekly active users, task completion rate,
> something else?"
>
> **User answer:** "If 40% of users who see the new onboarding flow
> complete it within their first session."
>
> **Proposed text** (replacing "improve user engagement"): *"Success
> metric: 40% of users who are shown the new onboarding flow complete it
> within their first session, measured over the first 30 days post-launch."*
>
> User approves → the PRD file is edited to replace the vague line with
> this one.`;
export const splitterBreakdown = `## Step 3: Propose the breakdown

Map the PRD to issues using this default rule, adapting to the PRD's actual
structure rather than forcing it:

- **Epic** — title drawn from the PRD's title or problem statement; the
  description starts from the problem statement/context section. Since an
  uploaded or pasted PRD has no stable URL to link back to, ask the user
  whether the Epic description should include the full PRD text or just a
  summary plus a "Source: <PRD name>" note.
- **Story** — one per major feature or requirement section in the PRD. The
  description is that section's content; any acceptance-criteria bullets in
  the PRD become an explicit "Acceptance Criteria" list in the issue
  description. Each Story is created as a child of the Epic.
- **Task** — one per explicit non-functional or technical requirement
  (performance, security, data handling, infra, migration work). Linked to
  the Epic; if the PRD ties the requirement to one specific feature, note
  the related Story by name in the Task's description rather than nesting
  it, unless the project's issue-type metadata confirms sub-tasks are
  actually available — only use a literal sub-task type if it's confirmed
  to exist.
- **Not turned into issues**: sections like "Out of scope" or "Open
  questions" are folded into the Epic description as context. Open
  questions especially should be called out explicitly in the Epic
  description so they read as unresolved, not as commitments.

Present the full proposed list — Epic title, then each Story/Task title
with a one-line summary — before creating anything.`;
